---
/*  What the decoder adds to the window every plugin shares: its outputs in the header, the headphones' and the
    speakers' pages, the loudspeaker table, the orientation strip where the effects have their matrix, and its
    scene. The pictures are the decoder's own, drawn by its picture tool. */
import TutorialPage from '../../layouts/TutorialPage.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const img = (name: string) => `${base}/tutorial/${name}.webp`;
const window = `${base}/tutorial/the-window`;

const parts: [string, string][] = [
  ['outputs', 'outputs'],
  ['headphones', 'headphones'],
  ['speakers', 'speakers'],
  ['decoding', 'decoding'],
  ['fill', 'fill'],
  ['loudspeakers', 'loudspeakers'],
  ['orientation', 'orientation'],
  ['scene', 'the 3D view'],
  ['try', 'things to try'],
];
---

<TutorialPage
  id="decoder"
  sub="ambisonics out to loudspeakers or headphones, with head tracking"
  parts={parts}
  window={{ src: img('decoder-window'), alt: 'A decoder window: headphones and speakers in the header, the 3D view with the head and the loudspeakers, the orientation strip, and the headphones page.', caption: 'a decoder window' }}
>
  <div class="prose" slot="intro">
    <p class="lede">
      The decoder plays ambisonics on headphones as binaural, on loudspeakers, or on both at once, with head
      tracking.
    </p>
    <p>
      Its window is the one every bambi plugin has, with more of it its own than in any other plugin: outputs in
      the header, and head tracking where the others have their matrix. The 3D view, presets and the link are explained
      in <a href={window}>the window</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="outputs">
    <h2>outputs</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('decoder-outputs')} alt="The header's outputs: headphones open, speakers beside it, each with its box." width="640" height="88" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Where the other plugins have their instances, the decoder's header has its two outputs,
        <code>headphones</code> and <code>speakers</code>; with another decoder in the session, a switch beside them
        shows and edits that one. <strong>The box</strong> switches an output on; <strong>the name</strong> opens its
        page in the panel. Both can play at once, each on channels of its own. If both are set to the same channels,
        the page warns you and says what would happen on playback.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="headphones" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-headphones')} alt="The headphones page: model, ku100 or sofa file; head and ears; the channel pair; through the layout." width="696" height="720" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>headphones</h2>
      <p>The HRTF used for binaural playback:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>model</code></strong> is an HRTF learned from measured heads, and two sliders personalise it:
          <code>head</code>, 0.75 to 1.3 ×, scales the time and low-frequency level difference between the ears, and
          <code>ears</code>, ±2 erb, shifts the spectral notches of the ears.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>ku100</code></strong> is a measured dummy-head HRTF, and <strong><code>sofa file</code></strong>
          loads any measured HRTF. With a measured HRTF the ears stay as measured, and <code>head</code> scales only
          the time difference.
        </li>
        <li><strong>channels</strong>: the pair of host channels the headphones play on.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>through the layout</code></strong> plays the speaker layout virtually on the headphones, so you
          can hear the loudspeakers without them.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="speakers" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-speakers')} alt="The speakers page on its layout sub-tab: itu, file or typed; ten layouts with 4+7+0 chosen; loudspeakers…; the channels and what plays on each." width="696" height="720" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>speakers</h2>
      <p>
        The speakers page opens on three sub-tabs: <strong><code>layout</code></strong>, below,
        <strong><code>decoding</code></strong> and <strong><code>fill</code></strong>, <a href="#decoding">next</a>.
        On <code>layout</code>, the loudspeaker layout is set in one of three ways:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>itu</code></strong>: ten layouts built in, from <code>0+2+0</code> to <code>9+10+3</code>.</li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>file</code></strong>: an IEM AllRADecoder or AmbDec layout, decoded by bambi, or a complete
          decoder as SOFA filters, used as is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>typed</code></strong>: the <a href="#loudspeakers">table</a> is the layout. A loaded one
          becomes typed the moment you edit it there; a new one starts empty.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong><code>loudspeakers…</code></strong> opens the <a href="#loudspeakers">table</a>. <strong>channels</strong>
        shows the bus's channels and what plays on each, and a line names each output's channels: the layout's move
        as a block, so a headphone pair or an interface's own outputs can come before them. An output that does not
        fit is marked <code>not playing</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="decoding" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-decoding')} alt="The decoding sub-tab: one listener or audience, and distance compensation." width="696" height="280" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>decoding</h2>
      <p>
        Whether the decoding is weighted for <code>one listener</code> in the sweet spot or an <code>audience</code>,
        and <code>distance compensation</code>, which gives every loudspeaker the gain and delay that bring it to the
        farthest one's distance. A layout of two loudspeakers has its own choice instead of the weighting:
        <code>fold</code> puts the whole sound scene on the pair, and <code>slice</code> plays only what the listener
        faces, between them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="fill" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-fill')} alt="The fill sub-tab: fill the gaps binaurally switched on with its channel separation, the hrtf, and the tiles for the crossfade and the crosstalk cancellation." width="696" height="1000" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>fill</h2>
      <p>
        Most layouts leave part of the sphere uncovered: a stereo pair everything outside the arc between its two
        loudspeakers, a 5.1 or 7.1 ring what is above and below, a dome what is below. <strong><code>fill the gaps binaurally</code></strong> plays those directions
        binaurally on the same loudspeakers, through crosstalk cancellation, so each ear hears its own channel. What the
        layout covers plays exactly as before. It is for one listener in the sweet spot, facing the layout as it was
        measured, and it is off by default.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The channel separation</strong>: with the fill on, the line under the switch shows how well the
          loudspeakers keep each ear’s channel out of the other ear. Below 25 dB (all loudspeakers on one side, or on the
          median plane) the fill is refused, and the line says so.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>hrtf</code></strong>: the HRTF the binaural part uses, the same choice and sliders as on the
          headphones page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>crossfade</strong>: within <code>no fill within</code> of the covered directions only the loudspeaker
          decode plays (20°); <code>crossfade width</code> is how wide the fade to binaural is beyond that (60°).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>crosstalk cancellation</strong>: <code>depth</code> is how hard it cancels (25 dB): less sounds closer
          to plain binaural on loudspeakers, more relies more on the listener sitting exactly in place.
          <code>up to</code> is the highest frequency it cancels (4.5 kHz): above about 6 kHz the cancellation of a
          narrow pair rings.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        With the fill on, the decoder's latency is 256 samples instead of 64, on every output, and your DAW is told.
        The fill keeps the level: switching it changes the sound, not how loud it is. A pair whose gaps are filled
        plays its <code>fold</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="loudspeakers" class="split">
    <figure class="panel">
      <img src={img('decoder-loudspeakers')} alt="The loudspeakers table: a row each with its azimuth, elevation, distance, gain, delay and a test button." width="696" height="820" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>loudspeakers</h2>
      <p><code>loudspeakers…</code> opens the table, one loudspeaker to a row:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Drag a number</strong> as you drag a tile: its channel, azimuth, elevation and distance. Gain and
          delay are what distance compensation gives it, and are read only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The box at the left</strong> makes a loudspeaker imaginary: it shapes the layout but is never fed.
        </li>
        <li><strong><code>test</code></strong> sends a burst on that loudspeaker's channel alone, so you can check each by ear.</li>
        <li><strong>The cross</strong> removes the row.</li>
        <li><strong><code>add a loudspeaker</code></strong> adds one ahead, at the farthest distance, on the next free channel.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="orientation">
    <h2>orientation</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('decoder-orientation')} alt="The orientation strip: none, osc, airpods and phone; recentre and setup; yaw, pitch and roll, each with a flip, and their rates." width="1236" height="420" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Where the effects have their matrix, the decoder has head tracking, always on screen. The sliders and rates
        rotate the sound scene for every output; a tracker that tracks the head rotates the headphones, and a
        two-loudspeaker layout set to <code>slice</code>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The head tracker</strong>: <code>none</code>, <code>osc</code>, <code>airpods</code> or a
          <code>phone</code>. AirPods and a phone connect through a helper app that comes with the decoder, on macOS.
          Beside the tabs, the tracker shows whether it is sending data, and at what rate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>recentre</code></strong> makes the direction you face now the front, and <strong><code>setup…</code></strong>
          opens the tracker's own page beside the output's: the OSC port and message, the AirPods' look-and-nod, or the
          code a phone scans. There it <strong><code>tracks the head</code></strong>, compensating your head movement so
          the sound stays put in the room while you turn, or <strong><code>rotates the scene</code></strong>, one more
          rotation of the sound scene for every output.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>yaw</code></strong>, <strong><code>pitch</code></strong> and <strong><code>roll</code></strong>
          rotate the sound scene manually, each with a rate under it. <code>flip</code> beside an angle inverts the
          tracker on that axis, and a double-click on an angle resets it and what its rate has added.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scene">
    <h2>the 3D view</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src={img('decoder-scene')} alt="The 3D view on the speakers page: the head at the centre, the numbered loudspeakers, and the convex hull between them." width="1236" height="602" />
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The decoder draws the listener's head at the centre — on the globe with its eyes on either side of the look
        direction, on the equirect as a cross at the look direction — and the numbered loudspeakers around it. While
        the speakers page is open, the convex hull between them is drawn too. An output that is not playing is drawn
        faint, and an imaginary loudspeaker dashed. The energy display shows the signal every output receives. Two
        switches are the decoder's own:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><code>head frame</code></strong> draws the 3D view from the head's point of view: the equirect's front follows the look direction.</li>
        <li><strong><code>sources</code></strong> shows where the session's sources are, rotated with the sound scene.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="try">
    <h2>things to try</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>A stereo pair that follows your head.</strong> On the speakers page choose <code>0+2+0</code> and
          <code>slice</code> on <code>decoding</code>, then a tracker that tracks the head: the pair plays only what you face.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Loudspeakers on headphones.</strong> Choose a layout on the speakers page, then switch on
          <code>through the layout</code> on the headphones page to hear it before you set it up.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Sound from above on a 7.1 ring.</strong> Choose <code>0+7+0</code>, open <code>fill</code> and switch
          on <code>fill the gaps binaurally</code>, then sit in the sweet spot and move a source over your head.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rotate the sound scene, not the head.</strong> Give <code>yaw rate</code> a value and press play: the
          whole sound scene rotates around you, on every output.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
</TutorialPage>
